PART I — PRODUCT AND PRD REVIEW
1. Executive assessment
The PRD has a sound product direction. The core idea is clear: a detailed, dark, analytics-first Social Media Monitoring cockpit for one personal brand (null), not another general-purpose productivity application.

The strongest parts of the PRD are:
1. A clear single-purpose product boundary: Social Media Monitoring only.
2. A detailed primary-user persona that prefers depth, source transparency, and drill-downs.
3. A strong visual reference with a recognizable analytics hierarchy.
4. A useful information architecture: Overview, Content, Analytics, Audience, Compare, Accounts, Sync, Analyst, Settings.
5. Good metric semantics: null is not zero; Views, Reach, and Impressions are not interchangeable; historical observations need source and timestamps.
6. An existing Backend and n8n automation architecture that must be reused rather than replaced.
7. A clear distinction between API data, CSV data, mock data, and unavailable data.
The PRD does not need another feature expansion. It needs a more deterministic build sequence, explicit screen behavior, stricter data contracts, and guardrails that prevent a low-effort coding model from inventing routes, models, metrics, or UI behavior.
2. What the visual reference actually communicates
The supplied reference is a dark Meta-style performance dashboard, approximately 1117 × 797 px. Its defining design properties are:
- A very narrow vertical icon rail on the left.
- A compact top bar with search on the left and platform/account selectors on the right.
- A page title directly below the top bar.
- A first row of four compact KPI cards.
- A dense two-column dashboard body, with a wide main column and a narrower right rail.
- A large audience-location panel in the main column: map at left, impression total and country percentages at right.
- A right-side content-type breakdown panel with compact segmented selectors and horizontal distribution bars.
- A right-side profile-activity panel with a short list of activity metrics and period-over-period deltas.
- A wide trend chart in the lower main column, with a Daily / Cumulative switch and a restrained legend.
- A small AI Assistant insight panel in the lower right, visually subordinate to the analytics.
- Near-black backgrounds, thin charcoal borders, compact type, muted secondary text, restrained white/gray chart lines, and no decorative gradients.
The target is to reuse this layout logic and information density. Do not copy Meta branding, labels, sample values, or account details. Do not reuse any numbers from the screenshot as real data.
Visual rules to implement
- Desktop-first layout at 1280 px and wider.
- Sidebar: narrow icon rail, approximately 56–64 px; expanded navigation may be wider but must stay compact.
- Main content: consistent 16–24 px spacing; dense panels aligned to a clear grid.
- Main dashboard grid: approximately 70% primary column / 30% secondary rail, adapting based on available width.
- KPI cards: compact height, aligned numeric baselines, label + tooltip + value + comparison delta.
- Panels: subtle 1 px borders, restrained corner radius, consistent panel padding.
- Text: compact but readable; use tabular numerals for metrics.
- Charts: thin grid lines, readable date labels, precise tooltips, clear units, explicit metric selection.
- Colors: near-black background, dark-gray panel surfaces, fine charcoal borders, white primary text, gray secondary text. Use one restrained accent for selected states; green only for real positive status, amber for warnings/stale data, red for real errors.
- No neon cyberpunk styling, oversized headings, oversized cards, decorative glows, random gradients, giant empty panels, or an excessive pill/badge interface.
- Keep the UI information-rich without making it visually noisy. Density must come from well-organized data, not from cramming text together.
3. PRD strengths that must be preserved
The implementation must retain these decisions from PRD v1.0:
- The product is only Social Media Monitoring.
- Do not surface Chronos, time tracking, Pomodoro, project management, GitHub engineering dashboards, knowledge graph, documents vault, invoicing, CRM, or unrelated productivity tools in the Social Monitor interface.
- Do not delete those unrelated systems from the repository or Backend.
- All six agreed platforms must appear in the product, with honest readiness and connection states.
- Overview is the default landing page.
- Content Library and Content Detail are core screens, not optional decorative cards.
- Analytics are platform-aware and time-window-aware.
- Audience insights render only when source data supports them.
- Cross-platform comparisons must not imply false equivalence between metrics.
- Accounts and Sync Center must show real connection and execution states.
- AI Analyst is evidence-first and must not invent metrics or causes.
- Social-only module toggles must hide/show optional product areas without deleting records or disconnecting integrations.
- Demo data must be visibly labeled and must never be persisted as real social analytics.
- The existing NestJS Backend, Prisma models, PostgreSQL database, Docker deployment, and n8n workflows must be inspected and reused.
4. Gaps and risks in the current PRD
These are not reasons to expand the product. They are implementation ambiguities that the build prompt must resolve.
4.1 Same-day P0 scope is too broad
The PRD labels many large areas P0: Overview, six platform views, Content Library, Content Detail, platform analytics, Accounts, Sync Center, settings, module toggles, and several Backend capabilities. If the model tries to finish all of them at equal depth, it may create many shallow, inconsistent screens.

Resolution: use the execution order in Part II. The minimum complete product flow is:

Social-only App Shell → Verified Data Adapter → Overview → Content Library → Content Detail → Platform Analytics → Accounts → Sync Center → Settings → QA.

Audience and Compare must have usable basic screens but advanced demographic maps and sophisticated indexed comparisons are conditional on real data and remaining time. The advanced conversational LLM is explicitly later.
4.2 The app currently has no connected social accounts
The user has not completed social account creation, Developer App setup, OAuth, or API permissions. The current n8n workflows have been built in mock mode only. Therefore a newly built UI cannot honestly show live social metrics.

Resolution: support three explicit data modes:
- REAL: read records from the existing Backend only.
- DEMO: show isolated fixtures with a persistent, unmistakable DEMO DATA indicator.
- EMPTY: show designed onboarding/empty states, not fake values.
Never mix Demo and Real records in one chart or KPI. Never write Demo fixtures into production social tables. A CONNECTED badge requires a real authenticated provider test, not merely an account card or saved handle.
4.3 Backend architecture is described, but not yet verified against source code
The supplied architecture summary mentions models such as PlatformAccount, IntegrationCredential, SyncRun, MetricObservation, MetricAggregate, Insight, and Recommendation. Those names are hints, not proof of exact fields or routes.

Resolution: inspect actual controllers, DTOs, services, Prisma schema, and migrations before writing frontend API calls or backend changes. Do not assume paths, fields, relations, enums, or endpoints from this PRD.
4.4 Social post/content storage may be incomplete
The known model list does not prove that a post/video entity exists or that MetricObservation can reference a content item. The Content Library and Content Detail require stable platform content IDs and metadata.

Resolution: inspect the actual schema. If the necessary content entity/relations are missing, add the smallest additive model and migration following existing conventions. Reuse PlatformAccount and MetricObservation; do not duplicate them.
4.5 Credential ownership between Backend and n8n is unresolved
The Backend reportedly has encrypted credential support; n8n also has its own Credentials store. Storing provider tokens in both systems without an ownership policy creates duplicate secrets and inconsistent refresh/revocation behavior.

Resolution: inspect the current integrations module and n8n workflow configuration. Choose one clear owner for OAuth token lifecycle. Prefer the existing Backend integration credential lifecycle if it already handles OAuth refresh safely; otherwise document the existing n8n-owned approach for this single-user MVP. Never introduce duplicate independent token stores as a shortcut.
4.6 Metric semantics need an explicit registry
The PRD states the right rules, but the implementation needs one source of truth for which platform supports which metric, unit, aggregation, and display behavior.

Resolution: implement a typed platform metric registry/configuration. Every widget and chart must query this registry rather than scattering platform-specific if/else checks across components.
4.7 Data availability must control the UI layout
The reference image shows Audience Location, Total Impressions, content-type distribution, profile visits, and link clicks. These metrics will not exist for every platform, account, permission, or date range.

Resolution: the design should reserve clear panel positions but render one of three states: real data, unavailable-with-reason, or no-history-yet. Do not fill unavailable panels with zeros or invented country distributions.
4.8 Local Backend and n8n Cloud have different network boundaries
The UI running locally can call a local API if configured. n8n Cloud cannot call localhost on the user's PC. The eventual n8n delivery path needs a secure reachable HTTPS endpoint such as a protected Cloudflare Tunnel or a hosted deployment.

Resolution: do not attempt to solve the Tunnel in today's UI build. Make the API client environment-configurable and show the n8n delivery state from verified Backend data. Do not hardcode or invent a public URL.
4.9 Module customization needs a narrow scope
The old product became visually cluttered because optional features were treated as separate giant sections. The PRD correctly limits toggles to Social Monitor modules.

Resolution: use a compact Settings section with six Social Monitor modules and dashboard widget preferences. Do not create a large Modules Hub landing page. Core data, account records, ingestion, security, and Overview cannot be disabled.
4.10 AI Analyst is not a same-day dependency
A reliable LLM analyst needs stable metrics, enough history, evidence retrieval, and a configured provider. None of those conditions is guaranteed today.

Resolution: implement a small evidence-based Insight panel only if time allows. The full conversational AI Analyst remains disabled/coming later until data and an LLM provider are actually configured.
5. Critical product decisions
These decisions remove ambiguity for the coding model:
1. App identity: NULL Social Monitor only. No general-purpose Personal OS.
2. Primary user: one detail-oriented solo creator/technical operator managing the null brand.
3. Primary screen: Overview dashboard.
4. Primary visual reference: the supplied dark Meta-style analytics screenshot.
5. Core daily path: Overview → inspect platform change → Content Library → Content Detail → Analytics → Sync Center only when needed.
6. Data truth: existing Backend is the source of truth for app records; n8n is the collection/orchestration layer; UI is a consumer of Backend data.
7. No direct UI-to-platform API calls.
8. No direct UI-to-n8n analytics reads.
9. No new Backend, database, or n8n rebuild.
10. No fabricated metrics, timestamps, audience attributes, or connected states.
11. No cross-platform total that claims unique reach or unique people.
12. No universal engagement rate.
13. No advanced AI until evidence-ready data is available.
14. One-day scope is a timebox, not permission to fake incomplete features.
PART II — DETAILED IMPLEMENTATION PLAN
6. Delivery strategy for Carbon 4 in Low Effort mode
The model should not be asked to perform open-ended product design. It must follow a fixed sequence with explicit completion gates.
Operating rules for the model
Token and change efficiency is a top-level requirement. Carbon 4 is running in Low Effort mode. Use a reuse-first, minimal-diff workflow:
1. Perform one bounded reconnaissance pass. Do not repeatedly rediscover the repository or re-read unchanged files.
2. Search for an existing component, route, API client, service, hook, chart, style token, or Prisma model before creating another one.
3. Prefer extending an existing file/component/service over creating a parallel implementation.
4. Make the smallest patch that satisfies the requirement. Do not replace whole files when a focused edit is sufficient.
5. Do not reformat unrelated code, rename unrelated symbols, restructure folders, or perform opportunistic refactoring.
6. Do not create duplicate screens, components, API clients, models, endpoints, or data stores.
7. Reuse installed dependencies. Do not install a package for functionality already available in the project.
8. Use targeted searches (rg, symbol search, route listing, schema model lookup) and inspect only relevant file sections. Do not dump the entire repository, large files, lockfiles, generated files, or full execution logs into the context.
9. Do not use multiple agents/subagents or parallel design explorations. Follow the single approved architecture and the order in this document.
10. Keep progress messages short. Do not repeatedly explain the plan or produce intermediate long reports.
11. Batch related inspections and edits. After a change, inspect only the affected diff and run the smallest relevant test.
12. Run the required final build/type-check/test set once after implementation. Do not repeat successful checks unless a subsequent edit could affect them.
13. If a check fails, inspect the specific error, make a targeted correction, and rerun only the failing check plus directly affected checks.
14. Do not generate lengthy documentation, comments, sample data, or alternate implementations unless required by the PRD.
15. Stop when the P0 acceptance criteria pass. Do not continue polishing, expanding scope, or redesigning acceptable components.
Additional safeguards:
- Inspect the current Git status before editing; never overwrite user changes or reset the repository.
- Keep the application runnable after each meaningful milestone.
- Do not create new models/routes unless source inspection proves they are missing.
- Do not infer API paths from model names.
- Do not rewrite the application shell if a focused route/layout change is sufficient.
- Do not build six copied platform pages; use shared layouts plus platform-specific metric configuration.
- If real data or credentials are unavailable, implement a truthful Empty/Pending state and continue.
- Run actual tests/commands; never claim they passed without running them.
- Do not claim a feature is complete if it is only a static visual placeholder.
